WEBVTT - Usability Slogans That Actually Matter (long cut), Kurt Dirnbauer, English

1
00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:02.290
Usability is not decoration.

2
00:00:02.330 --> 00:00:03.350
It is not guessing.

3
00:00:03.390 --> 00:00:06.820
And it is definitely not
just making things look nice.

4
00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:10.290
Good usability means that
real people can use a product,

5
00:00:10.330 --> 00:00:14.500
understand it, trust it,
and achieve their goals without friction.

6
00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:18.760
Here are ten usability slogans
every product team should remember.

7
00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:24.410
Jakob Nielsen wrote them down in 1993,
in his book Usability Engineering.

8
00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:26.050
They are still true today.

9
00:00:26.090 --> 00:00:28.670
One: Your best guess is not good enough.

10
00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:34.720
Designers, developers, founders,
and stakeholders all have opinions.

11
00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:36.860
But opinions are not evidence.

12
00:00:37.030 --> 00:00:38.830
Your best guess may be smart.

13
00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:40.750
It may be experienced.

14
00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:42.820
It may even be correct sometimes.

15
00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:45.350
Test early. Observe carefully.

16
00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:47.900
And let behavior replace assumptions.

17
00:00:48.010 --> 00:00:50.470
Two: The user is always right.

18
00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:53.240
The user is always right
about their experience.

19
00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:56.590
When users are confused,
they are confused.

20
00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:59.430
When they cannot find something,
they cannot find it.

21
00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:02.370
When they feel frustrated,
that frustration is real.

22
00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:04.570
We should not argue with user behavior.

23
00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:07.450
A user who fails at a
task is not the problem.

24
00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:09.730
The interface is giving us feedback.

25
00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:12.380
Three: The user is not always right.

26
00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:15.670
But users are not always
right about the solution.

27
00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:17.230
They can tell us what hurts.

28
00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:19.260
They can show us where they struggle.

29
00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:21.110
They can explain what they expected.

30
00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:24.560
But they may not know the best design
pattern, the technical constraints,

31
00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:26.610
or the long-term product strategy.

32
00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:28.290
Listen to users deeply.

33
00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:31.490
But do not simply copy every suggestion.

34
00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:33.210
Users reveal problems.

35
00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:35.070
Designers create solutions.

36
00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:37.630
Four: Users are not designers.

37
00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:40.830
Users should not have to
design the product for us.

38
00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:43.610
Their job is to use the system.

39
00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:46.820
Our job is to understand
their needs, their context,

40
00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:49.590
their mental models,
and their pain points.

41
00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:51.880
Do not ask only what they want.

42
00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:53.450
Watch what they do.

43
00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:56.470
Five: Designers are not users.

44
00:01:56.510 --> 00:01:58.030
Designers know too much.

45
00:01:58.070 --> 00:01:59.490
They know where the button is.

46
00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:01.130
They know what the icon means.

47
00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:03.210
They know why the workflow
was built that way.

48
00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:05.170
That knowledge makes them biased.

49
00:02:05.210 --> 00:02:09.830
The interface that feels obvious to
the team may feel completely unclear to

50
00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:11.960
someone seeing it for the first time.

51
00:02:12.670 --> 00:02:15.780
So never use the product
team as the only test group.

52
00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:17.980
Designers design for users.

53
00:02:18.020 --> 00:02:20.090
They are not the users.

54
00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:23.470
Six: Vice presidents are not users.

55
00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:25.310
Stakeholders matter.

56
00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:26.830
Business goals matter.

57
00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:28.210
Leadership matters.

58
00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:32.320
But executives are rarely the
people using the product every day.

59
00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:36.180
A vice president may have a strong
opinion, but that opinion is not a

60
00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:38.080
substitute for user research.

61
00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:42.540
The best product decisions balance
business needs with real user evidence.

62
00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:46.220
A loud voice in the room should not
replace the quiet truth from usability

63
00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:47.700
testing.

64
00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:49.830
Seven: Less is more.

65
00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:52.960
Every extra option adds cognitive load.

66
00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:56.000
Every unnecessary field slows people down.

67
00:02:56.110 --> 00:02:58.820
Every additional feature
competes for attention.

68
00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:01.050
Less is more does not mean empty.

69
00:03:01.090 --> 00:03:04.050
It means focused. Remove what distracts.

70
00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:05.740
Simplify what confuses.

71
00:03:05.890 --> 00:03:07.680
Prioritize what matters.

72
00:03:07.970 --> 00:03:12.130
The best interface often feels invisible,
because it helps users move forward

73
00:03:12.170 --> 00:03:13.210
without effort.

74
00:03:13.250 --> 00:03:15.320
Eight: Details matter.

75
00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:17.580
Usability lives in the details.

76
00:03:18.350 --> 00:03:20.380
Button labels. Error messages.

77
00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:22.460
Spacing. Loading states.

78
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:25.120
Form validation. Keyboard navigation.

79
00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:28.180
Microcopy. Small details create trust.

80
00:03:28.220 --> 00:03:30.590
Small details prevent mistakes.

81
00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:33.360
Small details make a
product feel professional.

82
00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:35.760
A confusing label can break a workflow.

83
00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:37.710
A clear message can save it.

84
00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:39.950
Details are not decoration.

85
00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:41.970
Details are usability.

86
00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:44.090
Nine: Help doesn't.

87
00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:47.550
Help pages, tooltips,
and documentation can be useful.

88
00:03:47.710 --> 00:03:50.640
But they should not be used
to repair a bad interface.

89
00:03:50.710 --> 00:03:54.680
If users constantly need help,
the product is probably too confusing.

90
00:03:55.330 --> 00:04:00.120
Good usability means the interface
explains itself, through structure,

91
00:04:00.190 --> 00:04:03.280
language, feedback, and clear interaction.

92
00:04:03.690 --> 00:04:05.650
Help should support the experience.

93
00:04:05.690 --> 00:04:07.940
It should not be required to survive it.

94
00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:11.440
Ten: Usability engineering is a process.

95
00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:14.410
Usability is not a one-time review.

96
00:04:14.450 --> 00:04:16.190
It is a process. Research.

97
00:04:16.230 --> 00:04:18.410
Prototype. Test. Measure.

98
00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:22.880
Improve. Repeat.
Great usability comes from iteration.

99
00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:25.280
You do not guess once and hope forever.

100
00:04:25.350 --> 00:04:28.640
You build, observe, learn, and refine.

101
00:04:28.810 --> 00:04:32.910
Usability engineering turns
design from opinion into evidence.

102
00:04:32.950 --> 00:04:33.970
These slogans are simple.

103
00:04:34.010 --> 00:04:35.930
But they are powerful.

104
00:04:35.970 --> 00:04:38.550
Better usability equals better products.

105
00:04:38.590 --> 00:04:40.300
Do not rely on guesses.

106
00:04:40.390 --> 00:04:43.240
Respect users, but do
not make them designers.

107
00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:45.870
Listen to evidence, not hierarchy.

108
00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:48.410
Simplify. Care about details.

109
00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:51.860
And treat usability as an ongoing process.

110
00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:54.000
Because great products are not just built.

111
00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:56.960
They are tested,
learned from, and improved.

112
00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:58.780
Usability is not magic.

113
00:04:58.820 --> 00:04:59.920
It is method.
